-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/23ccd3f3-5f8a-4b8f-8ea1-ee42deb1699b
-- title:
--   Theorem 3.1(1), p. 5 — ℙ_x(T_a < ζ) = H(x)/H(a) for the fundamental excessive function H (part 1; part 3 is theorem_3_1_3; part 2 is not formalized in this mission)
-- statement:
--   Let $E=[\mathfrak l,\mathfrak h]\cap\mathbb Z$ and let $(X,\mathbb P)$ be the Markov chain with state space $E$ generated by a Q-matrix $Q$ (not necessarily conservative) that is skip-free downward, i.e. satisfies (2.1): $Q(x,x-1)>0$ for $x>\mathfrak l$ and $Q(x,y)=0$ for $y\le x-2$. Assume all states are transient and fix a reference point $\mathfrak o\in E$. Write $G$ for the resolvent, $\zeta$ for the lifetime and $T_A$ for the first hitting time of $A$.
--
--   Define
--   $$H(x)=\lim_{y\downarrow\mathfrak l}\frac{G(x,y)}{G(\mathfrak o,y)}:=\begin{cases}\lim_{y\to-\infty}\frac{G(x,y)}{G(\mathfrak o,y)}, & \mathfrak l=-\infty,\\[2pt] \frac{G(x,\mathfrak l)}{G(\mathfrak o,\mathfrak l)}, & \mathfrak l>-\infty.\end{cases}$$
--   The limit exists when $\mathfrak l=-\infty$, and for $x,a\in E$ with $x\ge a$,
--   $$\mathbb P_x(T_a<\zeta)=\frac{H(x)}{H(a)}.\tag{3.1}$$
--
--   This identifies $H$ as the function describing downward hitting probabilities; it is the first of the three fundamental excessive functions of the paper.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: E = [𝔩, 𝔥] ∩ ℤ with 𝔩 < 𝔥 in ℤ ∪ {±∞} → E : Set ℤ with E.OrdConnected and two points x < y in E (𝔩 = −∞ iff ¬ BddBelow E, 𝔥 = ∞ iff ¬ BddAbove E); reason: an order-connected subset of ℤ with at least two points is exactly such an integer interval
--   - encoding: all states in E are transient → AllTransient E Q: G(x, x) < ∞ for every x ∈ E; reason: Norris, Markov Chains, Theorem 3.4.2, which the paper itself invokes on p. 3; an absorbing state has G(x, x) = ∞
--   - encoding: the Markov chain (X, ℙ) generated by Q (jump chain/holding time definition, p. 2) → transition, resolvent, hitProb, hitBeforeProb, exitDist, killedResolvent computed from jump-chain path sums with Exp(1) holding clocks; reason: this is the paper's construction (i)–(iv); the cemetery ∂ is represented by leaving E
--   - encoding: real-valued probabilities ℙ_x(…) → ℝ≥0∞-valued path sums compared through .toReal; reason: a probability is at most 1, so .toReal loses nothing
--   - encoding: 'Define H(x) = lim …' (the limit is claimed to exist) → a Tendsto clause along atBot when E is not bounded below; fundH defined with limUnder; reason: limUnder is a junk value without a limit, so existence is stated explicitly
-- source:
--   Loeffen, Patie, Wang, Fluctuation Theory of Continuous-Time, Skip-Free Downward Markov Chains with Applications to Branching Processes with Immigration, arXiv:2208.14425v1, p. 5, Theorem 3.1(1), (3.1)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SkipFreeFluct_MBI_Chain
open scoped ENNReal Topology
open Filter MeasureTheory

namespace SkipFreeFluct.MBI

/-- Theorem 3.1(1), p. 5. For a skip-free downward chain (2.1) on `E = [𝔩, 𝔥] ∩ ℤ` with all
states transient and a reference point `o ∈ E`, the function
`H(x) = lim_{y ↓ 𝔩} G(x, y)/G(o, y)` is well defined (the limit along `y → -∞` exists when
`𝔩 = -∞`; it is the value at `𝔩` otherwise), and `ℙ_x(T_a < ζ) = H(x)/H(a)` for `x ≥ a` in `E`. -/
theorem theorem_3_1_1 (E : Set ℤ) (Q : ℤ → ℤ → ℝ) (hE : IsStateSpace E) (hQ : IsQMatrix E Q)
    (hS : SkipFreeDown E Q) (hT : AllTransient E Q) (o : ℤ) (ho : o ∈ E) :
    (¬ BddBelow E → ∀ x ∈ E,
      Tendsto (fun y : ℤ => (resolvent E Q x y).toReal / (resolvent E Q o y).toReal) atBot
        (𝓝 (fundH E Q o x))) ∧
    ∀ x ∈ E, ∀ a ∈ E, a ≤ x →
      (hitProb E Q {a} x).toReal = fundH E Q o x / fundH E Q o a := by sorry

end SkipFreeFluct.MBI
